            <ArticleTitle>[Incidence of pain in elderly patients. A questionnaire survey of healthy members of a senior citizen meeting].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>153-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">138 elderly ambulatory visitors to a senior citizens club were asked to fill in a standardized questionnaire dealing with symptom pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Some 88% of those participating indicated that they experienced pain at least occasionally. 50% admitted to experiencing pain on the day of the investigation. For the most part, pain occurred during the day and most commonly involved joints and legs. A total of 75% claimed that they would, in principle, use analgesics. Of these, 80% admitted to taking painkillers at least once a week, while 39% reported daily usage. A clear preference was shown for tablets and capsules or suppositories over ointments and intramuscular injections, with drops bringing up the rear of the popularity list.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of the investigation reveal the frequency of pain in the elderly, and underscore the importance of providing appropriate pain treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
